      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think that a macro is reasonable as it does not impose additional requirements on the types that want to be marked as such.  We do have an `SkNoncopyable` base class that we used to use but style has slowly shifted towards just declaring private or deleting the copy constructor/operators for similar reasons to the macro being preferred here.\n\nMinor style note would be that maybe we drop the `()`? Then the usage would just be `SK_STACK_ALLOCATED;` in at the top of the class?\n\nMinor name nit might be to use something like `SK_ENFORCE_STACK_ALLOCATED_ONLY` or `SK_ONLY_STACK_ALLOCATABLE`.",
